# Structs and Plain-Old-Data

Prefer **structs** for small, transient gameplay values, and **plain-old-data** shapes over encapsulation ceremony. Classes are for identity and lifetime (MonoBehaviours, long-lived systems); structs are for data.

## When to use a struct

- Small value types (roughly ≤ 32 bytes, a few fields): positions, damage events, grid cells, timers, stat blocks.
- Transient per-frame data passed between systems.
- Elements of bulk arrays/lists processed in loops (see data-oriented-design rule).

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — plain-old-data struct, public fields, no ceremony
public struct DamageEvent
{
    public int SourceId;
    public int TargetId;
    public float Amount;
    public DamageKind Kind;
}

// ❌ BAD — class + properties + constructor ceremony for transient data
public class DamageEvent
{
    public int SourceId { get; private set; }
    public float Amount { get; private set; }

    public DamageEvent(int sourceId, float amount) { ... }
}
```

## Rules

- Mark structs `readonly struct` when fields never mutate after construction — prevents defensive copies.
- Pass large structs (> ~16 bytes) by `in` reference in hot paths: `void Apply(in DamageEvent e)`.
- Public fields are **fine** on data structs. Do not wrap every field in a property; properties are for computed values or enforced invariants only.
- No logic-bearing properties on hot data (getters that search, allocate, or log). Data is data.
- Never mutate a struct returned by a property getter — you're mutating a copy. Store it in a local, mutate, write back, or expose a `ref` accessor.
- Avoid `struct` implementing interfaces used through the interface type — that boxes (see allocation-discipline rule).

## When a class is right

- Unity objects (`MonoBehaviour`, `ScriptableObject`).
- Long-lived systems with identity that many things reference.
- Data that is genuinely shared and mutated through references.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] New small/transient data types are structs (readonly where possible), not classes
- [ ] Data structs use public fields, no property/constructor ceremony
- [ ] Large structs passed with `in` in hot paths
- [ ] No struct-through-interface usage that boxes
